  "answer_text": "**Yes, there was cocaine in Coca-Cola.** This is not an urban legend; it is a well-documented part of the drink's history. \n\n### The Original Recipe (1886)\nCoca-Cola was invented in 1886 by Dr. John Stith Pemberton, an Atlanta pharmacist and Civil War veteran. Pemberton had wounded up with a severe morphine addiction due to injuries sustained in the war and was looking for a \"temperance\" tonic that could cure his addiction, relieve pain, and soothe \"nervous\" ailments. \n\nHe formulated a syrup using two primary medicinal ingredients:\n1. **Extract from the coca leaf**, which contained cocaine (giving the drink the \"Coca\" in its name).\n2. **Extract from the kola nut**, which contained caffeine (giving the drink the \"Cola\" in its name).\n\n### How much cocaine was in early Coca-Cola?\nThe original recipe did not contain massive, pure doses of cocaine, but rather a liquid extract made from coca leaves. In the late 1880s, a glass of Coca-Cola is estimated to have contained roughly **9 milligrams of cocaine**. Combined with the caffeine from the kola nut, the beverage acted as a strong stimulant. \n\n### Why and when was it removed?\nIn the late 19th and early 20th centuries, public perception of cocaine shifted dramatically. Initially viewed as a safe medical \"miracle drug,\" it soon became associated with addiction, crime, and racialized moral panics in the American South. \n\nTo protect the brand and avoid growing public backlash, Asa Candler\u2014who bought the company from Pemberton\u2014began reducing the cocaine content. \n* **By 1903:** The active cocaine was largely removed from the formula, leaving only minuscule traces.\n* **By 1929:** The recipe was refined so that the drink was **100% cocaine-free**.\n\n### The Modern Twist: Coca-Cola still uses coca leaves today\nThe most surprising part of the story is that **Coca-Cola still uses coca leaves to flavor the drink today**, though they contain absolutely no cocaine. \n\nTo achieve its signature flavor, Coca-Cola uses a highly controlled supply chain:\n* **The Import:** A chemical plant in Maywood, New Jersey, owned by the **Stepan Company**, holds an exclusive, highly regulated license from the U.S. Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA) to import dried coca leaves (primarily from Peru).\n* **The Extraction:** At the New Jersey plant, the cocaine is chemically extracted from the leaves. \n* **The Result:** The decocainized, \"spent\" coca leaf extract is sent to Coca-Cola to flavor the soda. The extracted cocaine is sold to Mallinckrodt, a pharmaceutical company, which processes it into a legal topical anesthetic used by doctors and dentists for ear, nose, and throat surgeries.",
